Therefore, if \\(s\\ge 1\\) (at least one sunny line), the total number of\n   points covered by the \\(n\\) lines is at most\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+ (n-1)\\cdot (n-1)=n^{2}-2n+2 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n   \\]\n   which is insufficient to cover all of \\(T_n\\).  Consequently **no sunny\n   line can appear** in a covering family of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n5. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) diagonals\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n   \\]\n   are distinct, none of them is sunny, and by (1) they contain every point\n   of \\(T_n\\).  Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) must consist\n   solely of the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_c\\); therefore the number of sunny lines\n   is forced to be \\(0\\).  Since the diagonal family provides a concrete\n   example, the only admissible value of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
